{"title":"Concave function","text":"<div class=\"mw-content-ltr mw-parser-output\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><div id=\"toc\" class=\"toc\" role=\"navigation\" aria-labelledby=\"mw-toc-heading\"><input type=\"checkbox\" role=\"button\" id=\"toctogglecheckbox\" class=\"toctogglecheckbox\" style=\"display:none\" /><div class=\"toctitle\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><h2 id=\"mw-toc-heading\">Contents</h2><span class=\"toctogglespan\"><label class=\"toctogglelabel\" for=\"toctogglecheckbox\"></label></span></div>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-1\"><a href=\"#Definition\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">1</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Definition</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-2\"><a href=\"#Characterizations\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">2</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Characterizations</span></a>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"toclevel-2 tocsection-3\"><a href=\"#Tangent_Line_Property\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">2.1</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Tangent Line Property</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-2 tocsection-4\"><a href=\"#Derivative_Tests\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">2.2</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Derivative Tests</span></a></li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-5\"><a href=\"#Key_Theorems_and_Properties\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">3</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Key Theorems and Properties</span></a>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"toclevel-2 tocsection-6\"><a href=\"#Jensen&#39;s_Inequality\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">3.1</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Jensen's Inequality</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-2 tocsection-7\"><a href=\"#Operations_Preserving_Concavity\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">3.2</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Operations Preserving Concavity</span></a></li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-8\"><a href=\"#Important_Examples_in_Olympiads\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">4</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Important Examples in Olympiads</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-9\"><a href=\"#Smoothing_Principle\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">5</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Smoothing Principle</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-10\"><a href=\"#See_Also\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">6</span> <span class=\"toctext\">See Also</span></a></li>\n</ul>\n</div>\n\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Definition\">Definition</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Concave_function&amp;action=edit&amp;section=1\" title=\"Edit section: Definition\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>A real-valued function <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/9/6/c96dd6ec1dc4ad7520fbdc78fcdbec9edd068d0c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$f(x)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"37\" height=\"20\" /> on an interval <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/2/7/027f4a11d6090f9eac0ce2488df6384dad1263ea.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$I$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> is <b>concave</b> if for all <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/8/b/b8b5f7c09ccbae469901770e4bcca3ec37cedf9f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x_1, x_2 \\in I$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"77\" height=\"18\" /> and any <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/5/5/755ac08c51d4d85ccc05f52d7092d14614fca5e0.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$0 \\le \\lambda \\le 1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"79\" height=\"17\" />:\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/1/3/d13e7bdaeb18b11b86183d8187ee69bf2ce850c9.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[f(\\lambda x_1 + (1 - \\lambda)x_2) \\ge \\lambda f(x_1) + (1 - \\lambda)f(x_2)\\]\" width=\"346\" height=\"18\" />\nIf the inequality is strict for <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/f/4/df422c6b18075ca678b06136508455ce249856ed.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\lambda \\in (0, 1)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"75\" height=\"20\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/e/f/4ef3c5db17446ccdb8b4031b4864a9464b767989.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x_1 \\neq x_2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"58\" height=\"17\" />, the function is <b>strictly concave</b>. Geometrically, the chord connecting any two points <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/9/5/0956603b00ef8285ef68a5f36851cd27fbe8f683.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(x_1, f(x_1))$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"85\" height=\"20\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/b/6/8b6d9896cb34b6a218a8e5285293dc813ffb8562.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(x_2, f(x_2))$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"85\" height=\"20\" /> lies on or below the graph of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/b/2/bb2c93730dbb48558bb3c4738c956c4e8f816437.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$f$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"13\" height=\"18\" />.\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Characterizations\">Characterizations</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Concave_function&amp;action=edit&amp;section=2\" title=\"Edit section: Characterizations\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Beyond the basic definition, concavity can be identified through several powerful analytic lenses:\n</p>\n<h3><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Tangent_Line_Property\">Tangent Line Property</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Concave_function&amp;action=edit&amp;section=3\" title=\"Edit section: Tangent Line Property\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h3>\n<p>A differentiable function <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/b/2/bb2c93730dbb48558bb3c4738c956c4e8f816437.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$f$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"13\" height=\"18\" /> is concave on an interval if and only if its graph lies below its tangent line at every point. Formally, for all <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/6/d/a6d3762f463e9adc10c72550c1be8dbcf7cd879d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x, c$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"28\" height=\"13\" /> in the interval:\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/6/2/062ff79299a87dfef5b416f408097196d28f2b57.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[f(x) \\le f(c) + f&#39;(c)(x - c)\\]\" width=\"205\" height=\"18\" />\nThis property is frequently used in Olympiads to linearize a function to prove inequalities.\n</p>\n<h3><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Derivative_Tests\">Derivative Tests</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Concave_function&amp;action=edit&amp;section=4\" title=\"Edit section: Derivative Tests\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h3>\n<p>For functions with sufficient smoothness:\n</p>\n<ul><li><b>First Derivative:</b> <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/b/2/bb2c93730dbb48558bb3c4738c956c4e8f816437.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$f$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"13\" height=\"18\" /> is concave iff <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/c/f/4cfc32b0aa554c9d98af355dafe67283ad403820.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$f&#39;$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"14\" height=\"17\" /> is monotonically non-increasing (<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/f/f/3ffcbce555d3bacd2a8202ba5a06a9363d3d6fe8.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$f&#39;(x_1) \\ge f&#39;(x_2)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"121\" height=\"20\" /> for <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/4/1/6410bdfa176299c64df7ea8c8ec86523d79942c6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x_1 &lt; x_2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"62\" height=\"15\" />).</li>\n<li><b>Second Derivative:</b> If <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/b/2/bb2c93730dbb48558bb3c4738c956c4e8f816437.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$f$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"13\" height=\"18\" /> is twice-differentiable, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/b/2/bb2c93730dbb48558bb3c4738c956c4e8f816437.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$f$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"13\" height=\"18\" /> is concave iff <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/e/f/aef8a2917e4d25ead46ce14292f365ddd8717c8f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$f&#39;&#39;(x) \\le 0$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"79\" height=\"20\" /> for all <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/c/b/bcba95962df051ada9f0cacf1da35e7ce20e887d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x \\in I$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"42\" height=\"13\" />.</li></ul>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Key_Theorems_and_Properties\">Key Theorems and Properties</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Concave_function&amp;action=edit&amp;section=5\" title=\"Edit section: Key Theorems and Properties\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<h3><span id=\"Jensen.27s_Inequality\"></span><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Jensen's_Inequality\">Jensen's Inequality</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Concave_function&amp;action=edit&amp;section=6\" title=\"Edit section: Jensen&#39;s Inequality\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h3>\n<p>The most significant result involving concave functions. If <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/b/2/bb2c93730dbb48558bb3c4738c956c4e8f816437.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$f$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"13\" height=\"18\" /> is concave, then for any <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/6/d/b6d1429906e4a890eced37ef00d03d91ea4eb4ed.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x_1, x_2, \\dots, x_n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"102\" height=\"11\" /> in its domain and positive weights <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/3/c/43c129d78c08b3d9acb3cf53bc8cdc34dc5e1244.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$w_1, w_2, \\dots, w_n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"110\" height=\"11\" />:\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/f/7/7f7615d3d9d47fd2cd0cd8fedca1df572a034557.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[f\\left( \\frac{\\sum_{i=1}^n w_i x_i}{\\sum_{i=1}^n w_i} \\right) \\ge \\frac{\\sum_{i=1}^n w_i f(x_i)}{\\sum_{i=1}^n w_i}\\]\" width=\"252\" height=\"44\" />\nThis inequality is the foundational proof for many classical inequalities, including the <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=AM-GM_Inequality\" title=\"AM-GM Inequality\">AM-GM Inequality</a> (by taking <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/a/c/8ac437b09da7c98f3c56607086126fb55ef44f3b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$f(x) = \\ln x$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"88\" height=\"18\" />).\n</p>\n<h3><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Operations_Preserving_Concavity\">Operations Preserving Concavity</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Concave_function&amp;action=edit&amp;section=7\" title=\"Edit section: Operations Preserving Concavity\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h3>\n<ol><li><b>Non-negative Linear Combinations:</b> If <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/e/2/7e22dc33801e6362ee086696af40c4e0bde7ec0d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$f_1, f_2, \\dots, f_k$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"100\" height=\"18\" /> are concave and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/9/7/b97e1e1d37a7c88f58b97ecf2698ad752b9500d6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$c_1, c_2, \\dots, c_k \\ge 0$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"130\" height=\"17\" />, then <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/6/1/7611a8a35f5ff4d12216dd84432f954160439c4f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\sum c_i f_i$\" style=\"vertical-align: -11px\" width=\"58\" height=\"31\" /> is concave.</li>\n<li><b>Pointwise Minimum:</b> If <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/4/a/54a85447e06ab22e1b385422ae53f9ea23930ccf.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\{f_\\alpha\\}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"38\" height=\"20\" /> is a collection of concave functions, then <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/4/8/7489868b1d4c0aa8d7b5717cc87d11071c5654b1.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$g(x) = \\inf_\\alpha f_\\alpha(x)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"128\" height=\"28\" /> is concave.</li>\n<li><b>Composition:</b> If <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/1/1/311cabda3a9b09f0dde217303ca9d1cd9201dcf6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$g$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"11\" height=\"13\" /> is concave and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/b/2/bb2c93730dbb48558bb3c4738c956c4e8f816437.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$f$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"13\" height=\"18\" /> is concave and non-decreasing, then <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/9/9/399702d66db837a0c84690ef1b7f608886e33128.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$f(g(x))$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"61\" height=\"20\" /> is concave.</li></ol>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Important_Examples_in_Olympiads\">Important Examples in Olympiads</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Concave_function&amp;action=edit&amp;section=8\" title=\"Edit section: Important Examples in Olympiads\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>The following functions are staples for inequality problems due to their concavity:\n</p>\n<ul><li><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/a/c/8ac437b09da7c98f3c56607086126fb55ef44f3b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$f(x) = \\ln x$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"88\" height=\"18\" /> on <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/b/0/2b0e4940d43e6b6313ba59e160cf570f79483a9b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(0, \\infty)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"51\" height=\"20\" /> (Used for products/powers).</li>\n<li><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/b/c/1bc9eec47d628562a54108d1717ea9298415c317.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$f(x) = \\sin x$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"95\" height=\"18\" /> on <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/a/1/0a151f5b93ce5735f72cdd79c46051f52b9d820d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$[0, \\pi]$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"36\" height=\"18\" /> (Used for triangle geometry problems).</li>\n<li><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/b/e/4be7337c33b33a68d3921eaf22f466ca51b2f977.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$f(x) = \\sqrt[n]{x}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"89\" height=\"22\" /> on <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/8/6/a86f243b13e6ed9b3b2934d2dd9024dc39cdc6b8.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$[0, \\infty)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"49\" height=\"20\" /> for <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/9/7/897470a282b6b85fa5221eacd41369b9a157f491.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n \\ge 1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"43\" height=\"14\" />.</li>\n<li><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/4/a/74a2bfd760dc00ae26fd31219bcea5614a8721c8.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$f(x) = x^a$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"77\" height=\"18\" /> where <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/3/e/f3e0b7b366964eea0ab30476faf1846b44e3f27f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$0 &lt; a &lt; 1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"78\" height=\"15\" />.</li></ul>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Smoothing_Principle\">Smoothing Principle</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Concave_function&amp;action=edit&amp;section=9\" title=\"Edit section: Smoothing Principle\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>For a concave function <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/b/2/bb2c93730dbb48558bb3c4738c956c4e8f816437.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$f$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"13\" height=\"18\" />, if we have two points <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/c/a/ccada11db7b2b90693e2fac4f887a57fce6f96bf.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x_1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"16\" height=\"10\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/a/7/6a7d010bbff66a0c41e43310a51efbaa6bf63396.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x_2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"16\" height=\"10\" />, \"smoothing\" them (bringing them closer together while keeping their sum constant) increases the value of the sum of the functions. \nIf <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/9/8/49893a1c70c98e337d646880a5fedbd38710f4c5.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x_1 &lt; x_1&#39; \\le x_2&#39; &lt; x_2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"146\" height=\"20\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/0/c/d0c834a8ad9febc0056025e0de62ac5d03134b90.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x_1 + x_2 = x_1&#39; + x_2&#39;$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"142\" height=\"20\" />, then:\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/3/e/c3e248f422d3cd651a29cde8d5f66488b06f580b.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[f(x_1) + f(x_2) \\le f(x_1&#39;) + f(x_2&#39;)\\]\" width=\"238\" height=\"18\" />\nThis is a powerful tool for finding the maximum of a symmetric expression.\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"See_Also\">See Also</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Concave_function&amp;action=edit&amp;section=10\" title=\"Edit section: See Also\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<ul><li><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Convex_function\" title=\"Convex function\">Convex function</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Jensen%27s_Inequality\" title=\"Jensen&#39;s Inequality\">Jensen's Inequality</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Karamata%27s_Inequality\" title=\"Karamata&#39;s Inequality\">Karamata's Inequality</a></li></ul>\n<!-- \nNewPP limit report\nCached time: 20261018081231\nCache expiry: 86400\nReduced expiry: false\nComplications: [show‐toc]\nCPU time usage: 0.025 seconds\nReal time usage: 0.033 seconds\nPreprocessor visited node count: 275/1000000\nPost‐expand include size: 0/2097152 bytes\nTemplate argument size: 0/2097152 bytes\nHighest expansion depth: 2/100\nExpensive parser function count: 0/100\nUnstrip recursion depth: 0/20\nUnstrip post‐expand size: 9060/5000000 bytes\n-->\n<!--\nTransclusion expansion time report (%,ms,calls,template)\n100.00%    0.000      1 -total\n-->\n\n<!-- Saved in parser cache with key wiki-wiki_:pcache:36801:|#|:idhash:canonical and timestamp 20261018081231 and revision id 284388. Rendering was triggered because: api-parse\n -->\n</div>"}